user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24-mr-gone","name":"Mr Gone","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2695"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-30T07:40:49-07:00","id":497439,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sorwen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/2645-star-wars-new-movies?p=496348#post496348\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yep. They were originally going to be Wookiees and he changed his mind.</div> </div> </div> </div>Didn't hit Mr.Gone's links, but I know it had more to do with budget than being a &quot;choice&quot;, as such -- even as popular as the SW franchise was then, Lucas didn't have the funds to do the Wookies as he envisioned so he &quot;settled&quot; for making the Ewoks.<br /> <br /> EDIT: hit the links -- brought back memories... <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have been around here for waaaayyyy too fucking long...","edited":{"userid":1433,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1433-just-john-forever","user":"Just John, Forever...","date":". 04-30-2015, 10:44 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1433-just-john-forever","name":"Just John, Forever...","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-03T21:18:20-07:00","id":502467,"title":"","content":"Not quite on-topic, but related, so...<br /> <br /> Friends and I were spitballing about how we'd remake the original trilogy, if Disney gave that job to us. Unsurprisingly, there's actually little we'd change, excepting more foreshadowing of the Skywalker family reveals (which means less incestous make-out sessions!)...<br /> <br /> One thing I came up with, however, stems from my little rant about bounty hunters above. The popularity of Boba Fett, as we all know, was a surprise thing, and led to him being awkwardly handled when inserted into the original movie. But it doesn't have to be so badly done, if he's built up from the start:<br /> <br /> He barely even shows up in A New Hope, but his reputation as The Dreaded precedes him. Greedo tells Han that he's glad he found Han before Fett did. Han blusters that the bounty on his head isn't big enough to even interest Fett...yet, before crisping Greedo before he can get a shot off. Fett is there during the confrontation with Jabba in the hanger, and the two have a cold stare moment...maybe there's some history between them, or maybe they've just heard of each other and are testing the waters. Han is visibly rattled, with a sense that they're going to have to settle this someday over blasters...<br /> <br /> Empire proceeds pretty much as before, as it's damn near perfect, anyway. Maybe a comment that Fett saw through Solo's garbage trick because he's been studying him, or something.<br /> <br /> Return plays out the same up till the battle on the sail barge: This time, Solo's getting his sight back and gets his hands on a blaster. As Luke leaps onto the smaller vessels, Solo notices a stillness in the desert air; there, down at the other end of the barge, is Fett. Star Wars is part Western, and it's time for an old-fashioned Showdown at High Noon. Though it's a desperate battle, needless to say, Han wins.<br /> <br /> We were split on how this goes down: Han kills Fett, fair and square, or maybe with luck and/or a clever trick. Han and Fett end up in a close-up Mexican Standoff, John Woo-style, from which neither might walk away...until Leia shows up and reveals that Jabba is dead, and Fett's not getting paid. At which he puts up his gun, and flies away, probably with a line about settling this later (Han notes that in the old days he'd have blasted Fett in the back, but he's not that guy anymore. The problem with Greedo shooting first is that it tries to make Han out to be more heroic than he really was back then; by Return, he's grown as a person, and really IS more heroic).<br /> <br /> One friend thought that Fett should actually show up during the final battle, either in space or on the planet, and rescue the rebels as Han did at the end of ANH, but I think that's a bit much...<br /> <br /> So, what do you think? The very notion of remaking SW is heresy, I know, and I'm not seriously advocating that, but one of the benefits of remaking a series is that you now know where the story is going and can properly set and examine certain themes that may not have been done so well the first time around. While I disagree with the guy who said that &quot;SW is a Western&quot;, it certainly is part of its DNA, and could stand to be emphasized a little more.<br /> <br /> <img src=\"http://static.tumblr.com/9ee78563ea1faaeef6a148730b1036c6/mqazwmi/sEjnka6dy/tumblr_static_9a8ea96ilukg0sco0w0ck4848.gif\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.